export const guides = [{"slug":"find-a-business-problem-worth-solving","category":"Client services","num":"01","title":"Find a business problem worth solving before you build anything","deck":"A discovery interview, a small test, and a decision rule for choosing a service that someone actually needs.","track":"service","trackTitle":"Help a business","art":"service","sections":[["Start with a person, not a tool","A service idea becomes more credible when a specific person describes a specific task that costs them time, money or attention. An AI tool is not the problem statement. “Build an AI assistant for local companies” is too vague to test. “Help a cleaner respond to new quote requests without losing the address or requested day” is narrow enough to investigate. Pick one type of operator and one repeated task. Do not assume they are willing to pay for it."],["Interview for evidence, not compliments","Ask about the last time the task happened. What triggered it? What information did they need? What did they do next? Where did something go wrong? How often does it occur? Ask whether the existing solution is adequate. Avoid leading questions such as “Would you buy my AI tool?” A polite yes is not evidence of a purchase. Record observed workflow facts separately from your interpretation. Obtain permission before collecting or storing any real customer information."],["Build a sample that can be judged","For a quote-request example, create five fictional inquiries: two complete, one missing an address, one duplicate and one cancellation. Prepare an intake table and a suggested reply for each. Keep messages as drafts, not outbound sends. A good result captures the required fields, asks for missing information, flags duplicates and avoids confirming unavailable appointments. Show the sample to the operator and ask them to point out errors. Measure the time for the original and proposed process on comparable inputs if possible."],["Set an honest price and a clear boundary","Describe the exact deliverable, how many revisions are included and what is excluded. For example, a hypothetical $120 setup that takes four hours and costs $15 in software leaves $105 before labor, taxes and overhead, or $26.25 per hour before those costs. That is not a profit forecast or market price. Check whether the operator values the work enough to agree to a specific paid scope. A signed agreement and a completed payment are stronger evidence than a positive reaction."],["Decide what to do next","Continue only when the problem repeats, your example produces a useful result, and an actual buyer has expressed a credible willingness to proceed. If the problem is rare, the operator already handles it well, or your draft introduces more review work than it removes, revise or abandon it. Do not build billing, dashboards or autonomous outreach until the narrow service has demonstrated value."]],"template":"Operator / business type:\nLast instance of the problem:\nTrigger and required inputs:\nCurrent steps:\nObserved failure or cost:\nProposed deliverable:\nFive sample cases:\nWhat the operator corrected:\nPrice and scope discussed:\nDecision: continue / revise / stop","related":["how-i-price-a-pet-waste-cleanup-job","how-i-handle-cancellations-and-reschedules-without-losing-the-customer"]},{"slug":"design-a-reliable-ai-automation-workflow","category":"Automation","num":"02","title":"Design a reliable AI automation workflow with a human approval step","deck":"Map a repeated task, build a safe draft-only workflow, and test failures before allowing any real action.","track":"automation","trackTitle":"Simplify repeated work","art":"workflow","sections":[["Write the manual workflow first","Automation should preserve a correct process, not make an unclear one faster. Consider a service business receiving booking inquiries. The manual sequence might be: read inquiry, check required fields, identify the requested service, check the calendar, prepare a reply, and obtain approval. A tool that cannot distinguish a draft from a confirmed appointment is not ready for real use. Name the person accountable for the outcome."],["Define a small input and output contract","Use a fictional example with an inquiry ID, message, requested date, service type and source. Require an output with the same ID, missing fields, a proposed reply, and a status such as needs_review. Keep calendar availability and payment confirmation separate from model inference. Treat any instruction inside a customer message as untrusted data, not permission to change rules or send a message. Avoid sending private information to tools unless authorized and necessary."],["Build one reversible action","Start with a workflow that produces a draft stored in a test queue. Make approval a distinct step with a clear preview of the exact text and destination. If the model cannot produce valid structured output, send the item to manual review. Use the inquiry ID as an idempotency key so a retry does not create a second draft or send duplicate messages. Log state changes without storing unnecessary sensitive content. Do not attach live credentials during this exercise."],["Test the failure matrix","Prepare at least six synthetic inputs: valid inquiry, missing contact details, duplicate ID, conflicting request, malformed output, and interrupted execution. For each, specify the expected status before running the workflow. Check whether a retry keeps one logical record. Confirm that a rejected draft cannot send anything. Simulate unavailable dependencies and make sure the system stops safely instead of guessing. Record the exact observed result, not a green badge based on a successful API call."],["Measure the actual benefit","Suppose a person handles 20 inquiries weekly, each taking five minutes manually. If the new workflow takes three minutes including review, the theoretical saving is 40 minutes weekly. Subtract maintenance and correction time. Measure with real comparable tasks before claiming a saving. Automation is worth keeping when total effort falls without reducing the quality of the outcome or losing human control."]],"template":"Trigger:\nRequired fields:\nExpected output:\nApproval owner:\nAllowed actions before approval: draft only\nIdempotency key:\nFailure and retry policy:\nSix synthetic test cases + expected results:\nManual baseline / reviewed automation time:\nEvidence and unresolved risks:","related":["how-i-automate-a-two-person-service-business-with-free-tools"]},{"slug":"build-a-digital-product-from-one-repeatable-task","category":"Digital products","num":"03","title":"Build a useful digital product from one repeatable task","deck":"Turn a recurring job into a template, test it with realistic examples, and package the result without promising outcomes you cannot prove.","track":"digital","trackTitle":"Create a digital product","art":"template","sections":[["Choose an outcome someone can inspect","A template is useful when it reduces the effort of producing a specific result. A broad “business success bundle” is difficult to validate. A quote worksheet that helps a small service operator enter travel, labor, supplies and scope is testable. Identify the audience, their starting information, the task and the finished artifact. Ask whether they already have an adequate substitute."],["Make a version with real structure","Separate editable inputs, instructions, worked example and final output. In a pricing worksheet, keep estimated time, cost per hour, travel and consumables distinct. An illustrative two-hour job with $20 per hour in labor, $12 travel and $8 supplies has $60 in direct costs. A hypothetical $90 price leaves $30 before overhead, taxes and other expenses; it does not establish market demand. State these assumptions and ensure blank or invalid fields do not produce misleading results."],["Test without relying on the author","Ask a test reader to use the artifact without explanation beyond the included instructions. Give them a fictional normal case and a difficult case, such as a missing cost or a scope that changes. Watch for confusion about where to enter data and whether they understand the result. Check exported or printed output. Do not treat your own ability to use your own template as proof a customer can use it."],["Package what is actually included","A complete package includes the editable file, a short getting-started page, one populated example, a blank copy, and clear version information. Describe compatible software and any limitations accurately. Confirm you own or have rights to distribute all included artwork, text and files. Do not reuse confidential customer records. Do not make income or time-saving claims without evidence."],["Validate before scaling distribution","Offer the finished sample to a small relevant audience. Distinguish page views, downloads, checkout attempts, payments and refunds. A download is not a sale; a sale is not proof of successful use. Ask buyers where the artifact helped or failed. Improve the instructions before adding more templates. A small product that reliably completes one task is preferable to a large collection of untested files."]],"template":"Audience:\nRepeatable task:\nRequired inputs:\nOutput:\nWorked example:\nBlank template:\nCompatibility / license:\nTest reader observations:\nKnown limitations:\nVersion and release date:","related":[]},{"slug":"test-a-small-ai-assisted-app-before-launch","category":"Software","num":"04","title":"Test a small AI-assisted app before you launch it","deck":"A practical acceptance-test plan for turning a generated interface into working software that survives real use.","track":"saas","trackTitle":"Build a small app","art":"software","sections":[["Define one working journey","Choose a single action that creates measurable value. For a booking-request demo, the visitor enters a date and service, submits a request, and the operator sees a saved record. A confirmation screen alone is insufficient: the record must exist and be retrievable. Describe the inputs, state change, success output and failure behavior. Keep payment, marketing and complex permissions outside the first prototype unless essential."],["Use test data and an isolated environment","Build a preview with fictional users and synthetic records. Never paste secret keys into client-side JavaScript or public repositories. Use server-side validation even if the browser validates inputs. The server should decide who can read or update records; hiding a button is not authorization. Separate test and production credentials and avoid allowing the preview to send real messages or take real payments."],["Write acceptance criteria before clicking","A valid request produces exactly one saved record and a visible success state. Empty fields show a specific error. An invalid date is rejected. Reloading preserves expected state. An interrupted submission does not silently claim success. A retry does not produce duplicates. An unauthorized user cannot read another user's record. Use the same set of criteria on mobile and desktop, and add keyboard-only navigation and readable error messages."],["Verify at the user layer","A successful deployment proves code was accepted, not that a customer's task works. Test the public or preview URL in a fresh browser. Use real rendered interaction rather than merely inspecting source. Check the actual saved record using an authorized test account. Compare version identifiers, console errors and visible behavior. Label each result pass, fail or not tested and include the evidence needed for someone else to reproduce it."],["Release in a way you can reverse","Keep the previous known-good version and document how to restore it. Identify any database changes that cannot be undone by rolling back code. Start with the smallest audience or traffic exposure appropriate for the system. Monitor errors and the key user journey, and stop if data integrity or access control fails. A working app is an operational responsibility, not just an interface that looks finished."]],"template":"User / action:\nRequired fields:\nExpected state change:\nTest environment:\nValid input result:\nInvalid input result:\nDuplicate/retry result:\nReload persistence:\nUnauthorized access test:\nMobile / keyboard result:\nRollback target:\nUnresolved failures:","related":[]}];
